[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" "question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" "question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" "question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115065 :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\det(A) =0$\n\nII. The system ![](images/8d/8dad0364549b8b3ea4a67136c845a979c1865ba0cc0ba8f673e247d8f26f4bf0.svg) has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/98/98b93ad401053c6e2be3d5899202b6de802d0b540406797a5285360ca23b6148.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" "question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115067 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\nII. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $\\det(A) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/60/60966175c795cda23b462c4dc57bb694acbd0ec5ce3ffda29cb20fba41a86d60.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/id #uuid "b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127912" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" "answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" "answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" "answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" "answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" :answer/id #uuid "36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer/id #uuid "56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" :answer/id #uuid "49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" :answer/id #uuid "a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5" :answer/id #uuid "e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/id #uuid "abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73561" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" "answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" "answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" "answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" "answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" :answer/id #uuid "e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" :answer/id #uuid "5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" :answer/id #uuid "974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer/id #uuid "e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384" :answer/id #uuid "c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/id #uuid "71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127916" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y=1 \\\\ y+x=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $7x+2y=1$ and $y+x=9$ intersect at exactly one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is not invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" "answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" "answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" "answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" "answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/id #uuid "83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/id #uuid "da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/id #uuid "1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/id #uuid "8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/id #uuid "c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/id #uuid "80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73560" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ kx+2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $x+y=0$ and $kx+2y=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. $k=2$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" "answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" "answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" "answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" "answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/id #uuid "5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/id #uuid "fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/id #uuid "34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/id #uuid "fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/id #uuid "fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/id #uuid "eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-31908" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" "answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" "answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" "answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" "answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" :answer/id #uuid "3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" :answer/id #uuid "8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer/id #uuid "8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" :answer/id #uuid "16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454" :answer/id #uuid "26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/id #uuid "11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73640" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. The system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement: \n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" "answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" "answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" "answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" "answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" :answer/id #uuid "e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" :answer/id #uuid "37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" :answer/id #uuid "06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" :answer/id #uuid "5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer/id #uuid "8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/id #uuid "3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-128155" :question/problem "Consider the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 2$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]=4-2k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point.  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" "answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" "answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" "answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" "answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" :answer/id #uuid "3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is not invertible" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" :answer/id #uuid "c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" :answer/id #uuid "754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has infinite solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" :answer/id #uuid "06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ are parallel" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer/id #uuid "8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186115065 :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\det(A) =0$\n\nII. The system ![](images/8d/8dad0364549b8b3ea4a67136c845a979c1865ba0cc0ba8f673e247d8f26f4bf0.svg) has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A^{-1}$ does not exist"] [17592186115065 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/98/98b93ad401053c6e2be3d5899202b6de802d0b540406797a5285360ca23b6148.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186115067 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\nII. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $\\det(A) = 0$"] [17592186115067 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/60/60966175c795cda23b462c4dc57bb694acbd0ec5ce3ffda29cb20fba41a86d60.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/id #uuid "b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2"] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127912"] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution"] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c"] ["answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" :answer/id #uuid "36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9"] ["answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer/id #uuid "56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741"] ["answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" :answer/id #uuid "49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505"] ["answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" :answer/id #uuid "a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b"] ["answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5" :answer/id #uuid "e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5"] ["answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/correct "answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/choices "answer-36adaed3-744c-5f09-bd8c-5ba73ad4e1a9"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/choices "answer-56dcedc0-5ad3-522b-a1b3-761011a72741"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/choices "answer-49fd4f01-d47a-5dd4-a111-cde834f62505"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/choices "answer-a7dc60c6-20e9-5772-9843-d46b6709a17b"] ["answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" :answer-field/choices "answer-e8522731-f8a8-5685-82cc-6976ace33ee5"] ["question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c"] [17592186115234 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2"] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/id #uuid "abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c"] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73561"] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions"] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e"] ["answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" :answer/id #uuid "e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684"] ["answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" :answer/id #uuid "5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b"] ["answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" :answer/id #uuid "974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2"] ["answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer/id #uuid "e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45"] ["answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384" :answer/id #uuid "c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384"] ["answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/correct "answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/choices "answer-e985e892-1f0f-5517-9df9-57134e2e0684"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/choices "answer-5c792dc2-a653-513f-99bf-36e6caec415b"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/choices "answer-974cde78-3995-556d-ab8e-57464a46abc2"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/choices "answer-e296bfa6-175b-598c-bbea-00b7833dca45"] ["answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e" :answer-field/choices "answer-c5bef56d-ae6a-5d52-937f-b6876b795384"] ["question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-5faf9002-9127-5900-9306-2ee7801daf2e"] [17592186115234 :knowledge-point/questions "question-abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c"] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/id #uuid "71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f"] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127916"] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y=1 \\\\ y+x=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $7x+2y=1$ and $y+x=9$ intersect at exactly one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is not invertible"] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb"] ["answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/id #uuid "83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7"] ["answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/id #uuid "da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a"] ["answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/id #uuid "1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb"] ["answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/id #uuid "8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4"] ["answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/id #uuid "c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6"] ["answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/correct "answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/choices "answer-83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/choices "answer-da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/choices "answer-1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/choices "answer-8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4"] ["answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/choices "answer-c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6"] ["question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb"] [17592186115234 :knowledge-point/questions "question-71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f"] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/id #uuid "80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa"] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73560"] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ kx+2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $x+y=0$ and $kx+2y=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. $k=2$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible"] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692"] ["answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/id #uuid "5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11"] ["answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/id #uuid "fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca"] ["answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/id #uuid "34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24"] ["answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/id #uuid "fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92"] ["answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/id #uuid "fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0"] ["answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/correct "answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/choices "answer-5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/choices "answer-fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/choices "answer-34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/choices "answer-fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92"] ["answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/choices "answer-fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0"] ["question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692"] [17592186115234 :knowledge-point/questions "question-80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa"] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/id #uuid "eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6"] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-31908"] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A$ is invertible"] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true."] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee"] ["answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" :answer/id #uuid "3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012"] ["answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" :answer/id #uuid "8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f"] ["answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer/id #uuid "8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38"] ["answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" :answer/id #uuid "16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a"] ["answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454" :answer/id #uuid "26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454"] ["answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/correct "answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/choices "answer-3358d599-0ad4-526d-9cd5-4a4d736e3012"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/choices "answer-8937e6e4-c93b-5db3-b786-4400eb967a2f"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/choices "answer-8a849500-99df-5a5d-9b11-3748b1ba1b38"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/choices "answer-16578899-abda-525b-b6f9-63812fd4252a"] ["answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee" :answer-field/choices "answer-26c47621-8db3-5de9-823d-303659412454"] ["question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-6bb66e10-1f1d-5f28-a12a-1aed917116ee"] [17592186115235 :knowledge-point/questions "question-eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6"] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/id #uuid "11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3"] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73640"] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. The system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution."] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement: \n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4"] ["answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" :answer/id #uuid "e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09"] ["answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" :answer/id #uuid "37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597"] ["answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" :answer/id #uuid "06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987"] ["answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" :answer/id #uuid "5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f"] ["answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer/id #uuid "8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d"] ["answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/correct "answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/choices "answer-e1ccae5c-81ee-5899-8699-e74123de4f09"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/choices "answer-37d3cd74-58c1-5b37-b36c-59bcc524a597"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/choices "answer-06b17d78-caf7-5e6f-a11e-6eddff4da987"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/choices "answer-5aebd097-c2d8-5750-b2ed-9feb5df4ca4f"] ["answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4" :answer-field/choices "answer-8fbca3c2-046e-5be2-b03e-4fcdd19d300d"] ["question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-382f4055-f58b-5b33-96f3-eda94c59e1a4"] [17592186115236 :knowledge-point/questions "question-11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/id #uuid "3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-128155"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/problem "Consider the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 2$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]=4-2k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point.  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11"] ["answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" :answer/id #uuid "3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344"] ["answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344" :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is not invertible"] ["answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" :answer/id #uuid "c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491"] ["answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491" :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular"] ["answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" :answer/id #uuid "754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435"] ["answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435" :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has infinite solutions"] ["answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" :answer/id #uuid "06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010"] ["answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010" :answer/value "The lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ are parallel"] ["answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer/id #uuid "8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5"] ["answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5" :answer/value "the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/correct "answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/choices "answer-3f61b2b9-4ed3-5f5a-93e1-77d09419b344"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/choices "answer-c8a1853b-53a4-53f8-b974-cbf7166d3491"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/choices "answer-754d60c5-a3ca-5827-8459-5e12bf352435"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/choices "answer-06fba96f-435c-539c-9a18-597700fe8010"] ["answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11" :answer-field/choices "answer-8dce1f23-3df9-503e-b55f-ae6c68e341c5"] ["question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-2fdf6a83-8af1-5f09-9e46-2efd4ed73e11"] [17592186115236 :knowledge-point/questions "question-3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4"] [17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)."]] "retractions" [[17592186115065 :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](images/8d/8dad0364549b8b3ea4a67136c845a979c1865ba0cc0ba8f673e247d8f26f4bf0.svg) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist"] [17592186115065 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/98/98b93ad401053c6e2be3d5899202b6de802d0b540406797a5285360ca23b6148.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186115067 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$"] [17592186115067 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/60/60966175c795cda23b462c4dc57bb694acbd0ec5ce3ffda29cb20fba41a86d60.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"] [17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y &={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)."]]}]